  <credit page="3">
    <credit-type>subtitle</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.241173" default-y="1123.016873" justify="center" valign="middle" font-style="italic" font-size="11.5">„Immer spricht man von der Cis-Moll-Sonate; ich habe doch wahrhaftig Besseres geschrieben.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Da ist die Fis-Dur-Sonate etwas anderes!“
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal" font-size="10"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic" font-family="Leland Text" font-size="18">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="7"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal" font-size="8"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic" font-size="11.5">“Everybody’s always talking about the C-sharp minor sonata; surely I have written better things.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>There’s the sonata in F-sharp major—that’s something very different!”
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="8"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Arial" font-size="9">_____________________________</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="3">
    <credit-type>subtitle</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="1114.75731" default-y="905.846714" justify="right" valign="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="9">Ludwig van Beethoven
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-weight="normal"> to his pupil Carl Czerny;
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>referring to Piano Sonata “à Thérèse” No. 24, Op. 78;
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>as recounted by Czerny to Otto Jahn in Vienna, 1852.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="2"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="7">Mus.Nachl. O. Jahn (Teilnachlass), folder 1: Beethoven-Materialien.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Berlin State Library, Musikabteilung, index DE-611-BF-10055.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="3">
    <credit-words default-x="1114.75731" default-y="1634.77119" justify="right" valign="top" font-size="20">■</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="4">
    <credit-type>title</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.241173" default-y="1583.670188" justify="center" valign="top" font-size="32">Sonata quasi una Fantasia</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="4">
    <credit-type>subtitle</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.241173" default-y="1457.940097" justify="center" valign="top" font-style="italic" font-size="18">alla Damigella Contessa Giulietta Guicciardi</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="8">
    <credit-type>title</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.241173" default-y="1601.218426" justify="center" valign="top" font-size="32">Notes</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="8">
    <credit-words default-x="85.725036" default-y="1549.793447" justify="left" valign="top">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Source material for this edition is the autograph
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[1]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> (henceforth “A”) and the original 1802 Cappi edition
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[2]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> (“C”).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Beethoven was involved in the editorial process of C. One can generally assume that differences to A are
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>intentional alterations from the author’s hand. There are also evident signs of plate corrections in C, many if
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>not most of which probably at the behest of the author. Even so, inadvertent errors and inaccuracies cannot be
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>ruled out. Therefore, this edition always considers the sources in conjunction. The 2013 Henle urtext edition
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[3]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>(“H”) is referenced when the interpretation of A and C is not straightforward, and any editorial decisions are
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>documented below.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The cover page of A is lost, so this edition has to rely solely on C for m1-m13 (read: 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">measures 1 through 13
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>“[A]” marks where this edition begins to rely on the extant A. Beethoven wrote two drafts of m27-m37. The 4
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>page of A (“A″”) comprises the revised version of a first attempt (“A′”). A″ is taken to be authoritative, but A′ is
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>also considered in order to illuminate the author’s intent where it is uncertain.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The engraving is based on the style of C, with concessions to modern notation conventions for readability:
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>augmentation dots are not placed at their metric positions, ties connect note to note, spelling of 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">cresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> and
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">decresc.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal"> is normalized. Conspicuous separation of voices is prioritized over vertical alignment, as done in C.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Staff distribution, stem direction, and beaming are reproduced faithfully to A and C.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Instructions
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">C capitalizes every initial s, including in the final “Attacca Subito il Seguente”, which A shows with both s’s in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>lowercase. (Also every instance of “Senza Sord(ino)”/“con Sord(ino)” in the 3
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>rd
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> movement: lowercase in A.) This
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>edition concludes the C editor misread Beethoven’s long s for a capital letter and puts the instructions in
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>lowercase. C erroneously uses q for g in “Seguente”.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>C shows prominent spacing around “e” after “delicatissimamente”. This might be intentionally separating and
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>thus emphasizing “Senza Sordino”, or indicating that “Si deve Suonare tutto questo pezzo” applies only to
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>“delicatissimamente” and not to “Senza Sordino”.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[4]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> It could also be a mere artefact of punching the lettering
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>by hand. This edition equalizes all spaces in text.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The dynamic instruction is given in italics as per modern conventions. C does not, though the first s of
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>“pianissimo” appears to be a long or italic s. This seems to be accidental; this glyph is not used anywhere else
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in C. “Semper” in C is corrected to “
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">sempre
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">”.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>A exhibits a conclusive “il fine.” in the ink of the notation, which is crossed out and above which the published
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>instruction “Attacca subito il seguente” is put in different ink, suggesting this was done at a later date. This
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>may indicate that the 1
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>st
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> movement had been initially conceptualized as a standalone piece.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>[4][5]
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> This edition
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>includes the crossed out instruction in brackets as a curio.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="12">Dynamics
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-size="10">The alignment of 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> in this edition reproduces the author’s intent as closely as possible insofar as it can be
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>deduced from A. H’s interpretation differs from this edition in m52, where it puts the climax of the swell at
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the 9
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text" font-size="14">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin" font-size="10"> instead of the 4
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>th
</credit-words>
    <credit-words> half-beat. A and C have 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> 
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text">
</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> intersect staff lines, slurs, stems, beams, accidentals
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>in various places as space demands. This is avoided in this edition for legibility, while the vertical position
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>above, between, or below the staves is not altered (but see m62/63 below).
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>m5, m42: A and C place the </credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Leland Text"></credit-words>
    <credit-words font-family="Edwin"> markings of the RH (</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="italic">right hand</credit-words>
    <credit-words font-style="normal">) melody voice in the treble staff, right before</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="8">
    <credit-words default-x="85.725036" default-y="63.92343" justify="left" valign="bottom" font-family="Arial" font-size="9">______________________________________________________________________________________________________
</credit-words>
